    "lawCode": "Amd §§104, 101-b & 63, ABC L",
    "lawSection": "Alcoholic Beverage Control Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S3567A\n\nSPONSOR: SKOUFIS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to\npermitting the sale or promotional gifting of certain complementary\nproducts for wine and spirits\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo allow retail licensees to sell complementary gift and promotional\nitems related to wine and spirit sales.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSections one, two, and three of the bill amend sections one-hundred-\nfour, one-hundred-one-b, and sixty-three, respectively, of the alcoholic\nbeverage control law to allow the sale of glasses designed for spirits\nand wine and the sale of merchandise in the promotion, gifting, and\nservice of liquor, wine, or wine products.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe gifting market in the United States is estimated at over $170\nbillion last year and is expected to exceed $220 billion in 2020.\nConsumers are increasingly looking for new and innovative gifts, partic-\nularly for adult relatives and friends. According to a recent Gallup\npoll, the average person spends $920 on gifts. Holiday spending for\nrelatives, friends, and colleagues alone amounted to about $660 for\nholiday budgets according to a recent media report.\n\nThe legislation proposed here provides consumers with additional options\nin the market. Under the new law, retail package or liquor stores could\nsell spirits or wine, which would include complementary items such as\nspecialty anniversary marking pens, salsa bowls, branded glassware,\ninsulated cups or mugs, recipe books, and other items. These items are\ncurrently prohibited to be sold under current laws and regulations.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nSenate\n\n2022 - S8826, Passed Senate Assembly\n\n2022 - A9685, Referred to Economic Development\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nIt is estimated the addition of complementary promotional or gift items\nwill increase sales and excise tax collection on wine and spirits.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nNinety days after it shall become law.\n",
